<section xml:id="de-crash-course">
  <title>A Crash Course in Concentration Models</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Many quantities in biology and medicine change continuously over time.
      Understanding how different government policies or administrative decisions 
      affect these quantities requires a way to model their dynamics. 
      For example, we might want to understand how hormone levels change during treatment, 
      or how they respond to changes in dosing schedules.
    </p>
    <p>
      Regulatory bodies and clinical advisors must translate these models into policies about drug approval, dosing intervals, and patient monitoring.
      This section uses differential equations and discrete dosing models to make those policy trade-offs explicit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Differential equations provide a way to model how these quantities evolve.
      In this section, we review the key ideas needed to study concentration
      decay, repeated dosing, and interacting systems.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="exponential-decay">
    <title>Exponential Decay</title>

    <p>
      A common assumption is that a substance leaves the body at a rate
      proportional to how much is present. If <m>H(t)</m> represents a
      concentration, this leads to the differential equation
    </p>

    <me>\frac{dH}{dt} = -kH, \quad k &gt; 0.</me>

    <p>
      The solution with initial condition <m>H(0)=H_0</m> is
    </p>

    <me>H(t) = H_0 e^{-kt}.</me>

    <p>
      This function decreases over time and approaches <m>0</m> as
      <m>t \to \infty</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <em>half-life</em> of a substance is the time required for the
      concentration to be cut in half. If the half-life is <m>T_{1/2}</m>,
      then
    </p>

    <me>k = \frac{\ln 2}{T_{1/2}}.</me>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="equilibrium-and-forcing">
    <title>Equilibrium and Constant Input</title>

    <p>
      If a constant input <m>u</m> is added, the model becomes
    </p>

    <me>\frac{dH}{dt} = -kH + u.</me>

    <p>
      This equation has an <em>equilibrium value</em> <m>H^\ast</m>, where
      the concentration no longer changes:
    </p>

    <me>H^\ast = \frac{u}{k}.</me>

    <!-- TODO: Add a worked example showing how to compute equilibrium for a medication that receives constant input, and a short check-it exercise. -->

    <p>
      The solution can be written as
    </p>

    <me>
      H(t) = H^\ast + (H_0 - H^\ast)e^{-kt}.
    </me>

    <p>
      This shows that the concentration moves toward the equilibrium
      exponentially over time.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="discrete-dosing">
    <title>Repeated Dosing</title>

    <p>
      When doses are taken at regular intervals, it is often useful to model
      the concentration just after each dose. If a fraction <m>a</m> of the
      concentration remains between doses and each dose adds <m>D</m>, then
    </p>

    <me>H_{n+1} = aH_n + D.</me>

    <p>
      This recurrence has a steady state
    </p>

    <me>H^\ast = \frac{D}{1-a}, \quad \text{when } 0 &lt; a &lt; 1.</me>

    <p>
      The sequence <m>H_n</m> approaches this value as <m>n</m> increases.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- TODO: Insert a short student exercise about when a dosing regimen converges and how parameter changes affect steady-state concentration. -->

  <subsection xml:id="linear-systems">
    <title>Interacting Quantities and Linear Systems</title>

    <p>
      Many systems involve multiple interacting quantities. For example, one
      concentration may influence the production or suppression of another.
    </p>

    <p>
      A simple model with two variables can be written as
    </p>

    <me>\mathbf{x}' = A\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{r},</me>

    <p>
      where
    </p>

    <me>
      \mathbf{x} =
      \begin{pmatrix}
      x_1 \\
      x_2
      \end{pmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      The matrix <m>A</m> describes how the variables interact, and the vector
      <m>\mathbf{r}</m> represents external inputs.
    </p>

    <p>
      An <em>equilibrium point</em> satisfies
    </p>

    <me>A\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{r} = \mathbf{0}.</me>

    <p>
      The behavior of the system near equilibrium is determined by the
      eigenvalues of <m>A</m>:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        If all eigenvalues are negative, solutions move toward equilibrium
        (stable behavior).
      </li>
      <li>
        If any eigenvalue is positive, solutions move away from equilibrium
        (unstable behavior).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="big-picture">
    <title>The Big Picture</title>

    <p>
      Across these models, a few key ideas repeat:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Exponential functions describe how concentrations change over time.</li>
      <li>Equilibria represent long-term behavior.</li>
      <li>Rates of change determine how quickly systems move toward equilibrium.</li>
      <li>Interactions between variables can be studied using matrices and eigenvalues.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      These tools allow us to move between equations, graphs, and systems, and
      to understand how changes in parameters affect behavior over time.
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>